0.13 (In this thesis, we present various scheduling techniques for programs expressed as) 144 466 P
4.48 (Synchronous Dataflow \050SDF\051 and Multidimensional Synchronous dataflow graphs.) 108 445 P
1.74 (Synchronous dataflow has proven efficient as a specification model for block-diagram) 108 424 P
-0.54 (based programming environments for signal processing. Two key reasons for its popularity) 108 403 P
2.67 (are that a\051 static schedules can be constructed at compile time, thus eliminating the) 108 382 P
3.64 (overhead due to dynamic scheduling, and b\051 it models multirate signal processing) 108 361 P
1.28 (application very naturally and intuitively. The first property is particularly important in) 108 340 P
-0.28 (environments where code-synthesis for embedded processors is desirable, since embedded) 108 319 P
0.7 (signal processing applications have rigid throughput requirements in order to meet hard-) 108 298 P
0.3 (real-time constraints. Multidimensional Synchronous Dataflow \050MDSDF\051 is an extension) 108 277 P
-0.07 (of SDF to multiple dimensions; in this model, applications such as image and video signal) 108 256 P
0.07 (processing, which operate on multidimensional signal spaces, are more naturally modeled) 108 235 P
(and specified than in SDF.) 108 214 T
-0.57 (The amount of on-chip memory available on embedded processors is often severely) 144 193 P
-0.62 (limited. Adding off-chip memory is usually not an option because it entails a speed penalty,) 108 172 P
0.87 (it increases overall system cost and size, and increases power requirements. Thus, when) 108 151 P
2.6 (generating software implementations from SDF specifications for a uniprocessor, the) 108 130 P
0.54 (scheduling problem of minimizing code size and buffer memory size becomes crucial. If) 108 109 P
1.5 (the scheduling is not done carefully, the blowup in the size of the implementation can) 108 88 P
