5.27 (preclude implementation on the processor. Previous techniques have focused on) 108 712 P
-0.33 (scheduling to minimize code size. However, this neglected the buffer memory usage of the) 108 691 P
1.54 (schedule; in this thesis, we develop techniques that jointly minimize for code size and) 108 670 P
3.05 (buffer-memory size of the software implementation. We give three polynomial-time) 108 649 P
-0.41 (algorithms: a dynamic programming algorithm that is used as a post-optimization step, and) 108 628 P
0.8 (two heuristics that use different approaches to constructing these schedules. The general) 108 607 P
0.85 (problem is shown to be NP-complete, thus justifying the use of heuristics. An extensive) 108 586 P
(experimental study is given to show the efficacy of all these techniques.) 108 565 T
1.4 (We extend these scheduling results to MDSDF specifications. We then tackle a) 144 544 P
0.47 (problem of a different type. A multidimensional signal can be sampled in many different) 108 523 P
1.31 (ways. A straightforward extension of one-dimensional sampling results in the so-called) 108 502 P
1.04 (rectangular sampling structure, where the samples lie on a rectangular grid. However, a) 108 481 P
1.78 (more general sampling structure is a geometrical lattice; sampling lattices that are not) 108 460 P
2.58 (rectangular can have many advantages in certain applications. For example, a signal) 108 439 P
0.14 (sampled on a non-rectangular lattice can have a lower sampling density than one sampled) 108 418 P
0.07 (on an equivalent rectangular lattice. For real-time processing of multidimensional signals,) 108 397 P
1.12 (a lower sampling density means fewer samples to process in a given time interval. The) 108 376 P
1.8 (standard MDSDF model suffers from the inability to model multidimensional systems) 108 355 P
1.39 (sampled on arbitrary sampling lattices; hence, we give an extension of MDSDF that is) 108 334 P
1.21 (capable of modeling such systems. The model we give preserves the property of static,) 108 313 P
-0.62 (compile-time schedulability. However, constructing such schedules requires the solution to) 108 292 P
2.23 (some challenging problems. In particular, we show that an augmented set of balance) 108 271 P
3.52 (equations have to be solved simultaneously in the extended model. The additional) 108 250 P
0.54 (equations are quite different from the usual balance equations in SDF and MDSDF; they) 108 229 P
-0.14 (involve computing so-called \322integer volumes\323 of parallelepipeds. This computation turns) 108 208 P
0.37 (out to be an interesting number-theoretic problem, and we present several approaches for) 108 187 P
-0.43 (solving it. Finally, we present a practical example of a video sampling structure conversion) 108 166 P
(system to show the usefulness of the generalized MDSDF model.) 108 145 T
