3.74 (Another trend driving this research is the advent of high-performance DSP) 144 649 P
1.07 (architectures that are capable of executing computationally intensive DSP algorithms in) 108 628 P
0.25 (real-time. These architectures are typically used in embedded systems like digital cellular) 108 607 P
0.52 (telephones, voiceband data modems, speech recognition systems, video compression and) 108 586 P
-0.46 (decompression, and music synthesizers, where constraints on speed, memory usage, power) 108 565 P
0.54 (consumption, and size are fairly stringent. The time-to-market of these products is also a) 108 544 P
(key issue in hotly competitive areas like consumer electronics.) 108 523 T
0.3 (Traditionally, DSPs have been programmed in assembly language by experienced) 144 502 P
3.95 (programmers, a tedious and error-prone process at best. However, because of the) 108 481 P
1.53 (constraints mentioned above, it is becoming more desirable to provide tools that make) 108 460 P
2.28 (programming easier so that designs can be conceptualized, optimized, and made into) 108 439 P
2.19 (products more quickly. The reliance on assembly language has been for performance) 108 418 P
1.93 (reasons, and one does not expect that automated techniques will be as good as hand-) 108 397 P
1.2 (optimized code in general. But we do expect that as DSPs become more powerful, and) 108 376 P
1.48 (algorithms get more complicated, it will not only become reasonable to sacrifice some) 108 355 P
0.23 (performance in favor of a quicker and cleaner design, but it will become imperative to do) 108 334 P
-0.21 (so, since it will become infeasible to hand-code large systems in assembly language due to) 108 313 P
(the high complexity, just as large circuits today can no longer be hand-optimized.) 108 292 T
-0.45 (The mass popularity and acceptance of the Internet is also driving the need for new,) 144 271 P
1.68 (throughput-hungry applications such as video-on-demand, video tele-conferencing, and) 108 250 P
2.04 (image and video signal processing applications in general. The real-time performance) 108 229 P
0.24 (requirements of high quality video are orders of magnitude higher than those required for) 108 208 P
-0.63 (high quality audio. Supporting these applications will certainly require multiprocessor DSP) 108 187 P
1.14 (architectures, and it is well known that programming parallel architectures is a difficult) 108 166 P
-0.21 (problem. A combination of having to program a parallel machine and having to program it) 108 145 P
(in assembly language is an even more unattractive one!) 108 124 T
