0.49 (This model of computation was proposed by Lee in [Lee86] for use in specifying) 144 551 P
-0.25 (multirate DSP algorithms. SDF is a special case of Karp-Miller computation graphs in that) 108 530 P
-0.04 (the threshold parameter is always equal to the number of tokens consumed on the edge. In) 108 509 P
1.2 ([Lee86][Lee87], algorithms are given that determine at compile time whether or not an) 108 488 P
0.14 (SDF graph deadlocks, and whether or not an SDF graph has a periodic schedule that does) 108 467 P
1.97 (not require unbounded memory. Algorithms are also developed for constructing static) 108 446 P
1.46 (multiprocessor schedules, where the partitioning and sequencing is all done at compile) 108 425 P
0.4 (time. SDF has since proved to be very suitable for describing a large class of useful DSP) 108 404 P
1.48 (applications, and its strong formal properties have motivated its use \050and some closely) 108 383 P
1.61 (related models\051 in a large number of programming environments for signal processing) 108 362 P
0.9 ([Lauw90, Lee89, Ohal91, Pino95a, Ritz92, Veig90]. Part of this thesis is devoted to the) 108 341 P
1.06 (problem of constructing static schedules that minimize the amount of program and data) 108 320 P
(memory required by the resulting software implementation.) 108 299 T
0.83 (If each actor in an SDF graph is required to have finite state and produce tokens) 144 278 P
2.39 (drawn from a finite alphabet, then the SDF model is not Turing complete [Lee96b].) 108 257 P
-0.25 (However, without these restrictions, the model is Turing complete. We will not be worried) 108 236 P
2.2 (much about these issues in the rest of the thesis since they are mainly of theoretical) 108 215 P
1.76 (importance. The presence of actors with theoretically infinite state does not affect our) 108 194 P
-0.16 (results since the optimizations we perform are at the SDF graph level, and at that level, we) 108 173 P
-0.02 (know that the buffers are of well-defined, bounded lengths provided that the SDF graph is) 108 152 P
(sample-rate consistent.) 108 131 T
0.07 (The term \322synchronous\323 in SDF is not to be confused with its usage in the context) 144 110 P
0.54 (of synchronous reactive languages such as Signal, Lustre, and Esterel [Benv91]. In these) 108 89 P
