1.67 (The Kahn process network model is an elegant model of concurrency that was) 144 684 P
1 (proposed by Gilles Kahn in his seminal 1974 paper [Kahn74]. In his model, there are a) 108 663 P
-0.02 (number of processes that communicate by sending data to each other over channels which) 108 642 P
0.19 (have potentially infinite queues. The processes execute concurrently; a process may write) 108 621 P
0.62 (to a channel whenever it wants, but if it wants to read from a channel and the channel is) 108 600 P
-0.47 (empty, then it must block until it gets data on that channel. In other words, a process cannot) 108 579 P
(base an action on the presence or absence of data.) 108 558 T
2.52 (Kahn is mainly interested in defining precisely the function computed by the) 144 537 P
0.06 (network. He proceeds by constructing a partial order of streams based on the prefix order.) 108 516 P
0.65 (A stream is a potentially infinite sequence of tokens, and captures the movement of data) 108 495 P
0.48 (along the communication channel \050also called the channel\325s history\051. In the prefix partial) 108 474 P
0.02 (order, a stream) 108 453 P
0.02 ( is less than or equal to a stream) 193.02 453 P
0.02 ( if) 359.83 453 P
0.02 ( is a prefix of) 383.53 453 P
0.02 (. Clearly, if) 460.93 453 P
0.02 ( is) 528.98 453 P
0.65 (a prefix of) 108 432 P
0.65 (, then) 173.26 432 P
0.65 ( has more information than) 214.56 432 P
0.65 (, and thus represents a more evolved) 361.13 432 P
2.39 (state than) 108 411 P
2.39 (. Kahn constrains the processes that implement blocking reads and non-) 171.78 411 P
1.95 (blocking writes to correspond to continuous functions on the prefix partial order; this) 108 390 P
0.95 (simply prevents a process from waiting for an infinite amount of time before producing) 108 369 P
0.82 (outputs. Thus, Kahn is able to use the Tarski fixpoint theorem to show that the function) 108 348 P
-0.17 (computed by the network is the least fixed point of their composition. Since the least fixed) 108 327 P
-0.04 (point of a continuous function on a complete partial order is always unique, it follows that) 108 306 P
2.15 (Kahn process networks are determinate in the sense that there can only be one valid) 108 285 P
-0.42 (computation \050that is, exactly one history on each channel\051 for a particular set of inputs. The) 108 264 P
-0.45 (fixed point in the network can be reached by executing the processes in the usual way since) 108 243 P
3.48 (Tarski\325s theorem tells us that the least fixed point can be reached by starting the) 108 222 P
1.2 (computation on the all-undefined state\321in the process network, this is simply the state) 108 201 P
0.26 (where all queues on the channels are empty. Also, the least fixed point of a Kahn process) 108 180 P
0.76 (network is usually a set of infinite sequences; hence, any execution of the network for a) 108 159 P
-0.42 (finite amount of time represents an approximation of the final answer. Put another way, the) 108 138 P
-0.49 (operational behavior of the network converges to the denotational meaning only when time) 108 117 P
(goes to infinity.) 108 96 T
