-0.3 (In this chapter, we have argued in favor of using large grain dataflow languages for) 144 678 P
1.11 (specifying real-time signal processing systems. We have concentrated on two particular) 108 657 P
0.53 (subsets of dataflow: synchronous dataflow, which has proved to be useful for expressing) 108 636 P
0.55 (signal processing algorithms, and its extension to multiple dimensions, multidimensional) 108 615 P
2.37 (synchronous dataflow. We have also argued in favor of block diagram programming) 108 594 P
-0.73 (environments, and described a strategy for synthesizing software from SDF/MDSDF-based) 108 573 P
2.81 (graphical programs. We have shown how scheduling plays an important role in the) 108 552 P
(compilation process.) 108 531 T
3.31 (In the next chapter, we review the SDF model in detail, and formalize the) 144 510 P
0.44 (scheduling concepts needed. We review the concept of single appearance scheduling and) 108 489 P
1.62 (describe its property of requiring the least amount of program memory when software) 108 468 P
1.1 (implementations are generated. We show that one particular scheduling problem is NP-) 108 447 P
1.07 (complete and that we have to resort to heuristics in general. We also establish a simple) 108 426 P
(lower bound on the buffer size required on any edge in a consistent SDF graph.) 108 405 T
0.13 (In Chapter 3, we identify the problem of constructing single appearance schedules) 144 384 P
3.16 (that minimize the amount of buffering memory required. We describe formally the) 108 363 P
-0.28 (buffering model we use, and contrast our buffering models to some other possibilities. The) 108 342 P
0.29 (buffering model we choose is shown to have several useful properties in contrast to other) 108 321 P
0.98 (models. We then build up some machinery to describe single appearance schedules that) 108 300 P
2.42 (minimize the amount of buffering memory required, and we give a polynomial time) 108 279 P
2.24 (algorithm for generating an optimal loop hierarchy, thereby minimizing the buffering) 108 258 P
0.45 (memory. This algorithm is shown to be optimal for a restricted set of graphs called well-) 108 237 P
0.73 (ordered graphs. Several extensions are given. We then tackle general acyclic graphs and) 108 216 P
2.64 (show that the problem becomes NP-complete. Two heuristics are developed, and an) 108 195 P
0 (extensive experimental study is given that shows the efficacy of these techniques. Finally,) 108 174 P
2.2 (we extend these techniques further to arbitrary SDF graphs, and conclude with some) 108 153 P
-0.57 (discussion of related work. Much of the work in this chapter was done in collaboration with) 108 132 P
-0.32 (Dr. Shuvra Bhattacharyya, a researcher at the Hitachi Systems Research Laboratory in San) 108 111 P
(Jose, California.) 108 90 T
