-0.41 (In Chapter 5, we identify a shortcoming of the MDSDF model, namely, its inability) 144 628 P
4.3 (to specify multidimensional, multirate signal processing systems sampled on non-) 108 607 P
2.84 (rectangular lattices. In a multidimensional, discrete-time signal, there can be several) 108 586 P
1.29 (choices for the sampling geometry; that is, the periodic \322grid\323 from which samples are) 108 565 P
0.84 (taken. The straightforward extension of one-dimensional sampling leads to the so-called) 108 544 P
-0.41 (rectangular sampling structure \050also called a rectangular lattice\051, and MDSDF is capable of) 108 523 P
0.41 (expressing systems dealing with rectangularly sampled signals. However, a more general) 108 502 P
0.39 (sampling structure in multiple dimension is a geometric lattice, and in general, the lattice) 108 481 P
-0.09 (may not be a rectangular lattice. Non-rectangular lattices can be useful in practice because) 108 460 P
-0.71 (the sampling density required to represent the signal might be lower than with an equivalent) 108 439 P
(rectangular lattice.) 108 418 T
1.27 (We review multidimensional signal processing concepts, and present a dataflow) 144 397 P
2.31 (model that is capable of expressing systems sampled on non-rectangular lattices. We) 108 376 P
1.7 (concentrate on static scheduling, and show that the problem of computing the balance) 108 355 P
-0.69 (equations becomes fundamentally more difficult. In particular, the balance equations in this) 108 334 P
2.28 (model include equations requiring the computation of so called \322integer volumes\323 of) 108 313 P
0.29 (parallelepipeds. However, we are able to develop a method that allows these equations to) 108 292 P
1.45 (be solved, thereby permitting static schedules. A practical example using this model is) 108 271 P
0.52 (given. The problem of computing integer volumes turns out to be an interesting number-) 108 250 P
-0.11 (theoretic problem and we give a partial solution to this problem at the end of Chapter 5. In) 108 229 P
(the final chapter, we present our conclusions and directions for future research.) 108 208 T
