0.4 (One desirable optimization criterion is the amount of buffer space that is required) 144 273.93 P
-0.6 (by the schedule. If we assume a model of buffering where there is one buffer on every edge,) 108 252.93 P
0.43 (then the amount of buffer space required on each edge is given by the maximum number) 108 231.93 P
1.42 (of tokens queued on that edge during one period of the schedule. It is easy to see that) 108 210.93 P
-0.1 (different valid schedules can have vastly different buffering requirements; for the graph in) 108 189.93 P
2.49 (Figure 2-1, the schedule) 108 168.93 P
2.49 ( requires 11 spaces in all, while the schedule) 306.45 168.93 P
-0.44 ( requires 5 spaces. This section develops a heuristic that attempts to generate) 176.99 147.93 P
