1.87 (The problem of determining the R-schedule that minimizes the buffer memory) 144 678 P
5.07 (requirement for a chain-structured SDF graph can be formulated as an optimal) 108 657 P
0.84 (parenthesization problem. A familiar example of an optimal parenthesization problem is) 108 636 P
-0.63 (matrix chain multiplication [Corm90]. In matrix chain multiplication, we must compute the) 108 615 P
-0.18 (matrix product) 108 594 P
-0.18 (, assuming that the dimensions of the matrices are compatible) 245.69 594 P
1.65 (with one another for the specified multiplication. There are several ways in which the) 108 573 P
-0.1 (product can be computed. For example, with) 108 552 P
-0.1 (, one way of computing the product is) 358.71 552 P
0.53 (, where the parenthesizations indicate the order in which the multiplies) 195.1 531 P
4.07 (occur. Suppose that) 108 510 P
4.07 ( have dimensions) 301.12 510 P
4.07 (,) 537 510 P
6.31 (respectively. It is easily verified that computing the matrix chain product as) 108 489 P
6.37 ( requires) 195.1 468 P
6.37 ( scalar multiplications, whereas computing it as) 273.49 468 P
0.93 ( requires only) 195.1 447 P
0.93 ( multiplications \050assuming that we use the standard) 287.87 447 P
(algorithm for multiplying two matrices\051.) 108 426 T
-0.35 (Thus, we would like to determine an optimal way of placing the parentheses so that) 144 405 P
1.9 (the total number of scalar multiplications is minimized. This can be achieved using a) 108 384 P
-0.23 (dynamic programming approach. The key observation is that any optimal parenthesization) 108 363 P
3.54 (splits the product) 108 342 P
3.54 ( between) 268.35 342 P
3.54 ( and) 339.7 342 P
3.54 ( for some) 402.47 342 P
3.54 ( in the range) 469.74 342 P
-0.05 (, and thus the cost of this optimal parenthesization is the cost of computing) 181.39 321 P
0.34 (the product) 108 300 P
0.34 (, plus the cost of computing) 229.73 300 P
0.34 (, plus the cost of) 459.63 300 P
1.8 (multiplying these two products together. In an optimal parenthesization, the subchains) 108 279 P
0.23 ( and) 171.71 258 P
0.23 ( must themselves be parenthesized optimally. Hence) 287 258 P
1.67 (this problem has the optimal substructure property and is thus amenable to a dynamic) 108 237 P
(programming solution.) 108 216 T
-0.03 (Determining the optimal R-schedule for a chain-structured SDF graph is similar to) 144 195 P
4.28 (the matrix chain multiplication problem. Recall the example of Figure 3-1. Here) 108 174 P
0.82 (; an optimal R-schedule is) 268.27 153 P
0.82 (;) 536.66 153 P
1.29 (and the associated buffer memory requirement is) 108 132 P
1.29 (. Therefore, as in the matrix chain) 369.64 132 P
-0.41 (multiplication case, the optimal parenthesization \050of the schedule body\051 contains a break in) 108 111 P
1.67 (the chain at some) 108 90 P
