-0.25 (In this chapter, we have tackled the problem of computing schedules that minimize) 144 560 P
0.01 (code size as a primary goal, with buffer memory minimization as a secondary goal. Code-) 108 539 P
0.49 (size minimization is achieved by restricting our attention to single appearance schedules.) 108 518 P
2.19 (Buffer minimization is achieved by factoring single appearance schedules; that is, by) 108 497 P
1.17 (organizing nested loops in the schedule. We have argued in favor of a buffering model) 108 476 P
0.36 (where there is a buffer on each edge in the graph. We have shown that when the graph is) 108 455 P
0.64 (well-ordered, meaning there is only one topological sort, the problem of finding a single) 108 434 P
0.28 (appearance schedule that minimizes the total buffer memory can be solved in polynomial) 108 413 P
2.06 (time by a dynamic programming algorithm. We show that the dynamic programming) 108 392 P
-0.17 (algorithm can be extended in several interesting ways to apply to arbitrary schedules \050both) 108 371 P
0.93 (single appearance and non-single appearance\051. We also show how it can be extended to) 108 350 P
-0.02 (apply to a restricted model of buffer sharing. We have shown that the buffer minimization) 108 329 P
0.86 (problem becomes NP-complete for general acyclic graphs, and hence heuristics must be) 108 308 P
-0.25 (used. We give two heuristics: RPMC, and APGAN, and present an extensive experimental) 108 287 P
0.75 (study to demonstrate the strengths and weaknesses of these two heuristics. The APGAN) 108 266 P
0.45 (heuristic is optimal for a certain class of acyclic SDF graphs; this class is shown to be of) 108 245 P
0.54 (practical value since several practical SDF systems fall into the class. Finally, we extend) 108 224 P
(all of the algorithms to apply to cyclic graphs in certain restricted ways.) 108 203 T
4.41 (These algorithms have all been implemented in the Ptolemy programming) 144 182 P
-0.19 (environment. The APGAN approach has been implemented by the Alta Group of Cadence) 108 161 P
(Design Systems Inc. in their Signal Processing Worksystem programming environment.) 108 140 T
