-0.09 (In [Watl95], Watlington and Bove discuss a stream-based computing paradigm for) 144 602 P
1.29 (programming video processing applications. Rather than dealing with multidimensional) 108 581 P
2.24 (dataspaces directly, as is done in this chapter, the authors sketch some ideas of how) 108 560 P
2.78 (multidimensional arrays can be collapsed into one-dimensional streams using simple) 108 539 P
0.04 (horizontal/vertical scanning techniques. They propose to exploit data parallelism from the) 108 518 P
2.17 (one-dimensional stream model of the multidimensional system. While these ideas are) 108 497 P
-0.61 (interesting, it has been our experience that going to a one-dimensional model usually incurs) 108 476 P
0.9 (extensive overhead since, often, extraneous actors that merely rearrange data have to be) 108 455 P
0.65 (inserted into the system description to ensure correct operation. For example, in order to) 108 434 P
-0.33 (represent a 2-dimensional FFT operation using only 1 dimensional FFTs operating on one-) 108 413 P
1.25 (dimensional streams, actors that transpose arrays have to be inserted into the algorithm) 108 392 P
2.95 ([Chen94]. These actors are not only an unnecessary computational burden, but also) 108 371 P
-0.06 (obfuscate the semantics of the algorithm since they are an artifact of the model being used) 108 350 P
1.13 (and not inherently part of the algorithm itself. Watlington and Bove also propose using) 108 329 P
0.04 (dynamic \050run-time\051 scheduling for their systems. However, our experience suggests that a) 108 308 P
-0.64 (great many signal processing systems can be modeled using SDF and MDSDF graphs since) 108 287 P
0.28 (they seldom involve any data-dependent actors or control operations; these graphs can be) 108 266 P
0.01 (scheduled statically. Systems that do involve control actors and data-dependent actors can) 108 245 P
-0.3 (be modeled by the more general boolean dataflow \050BDF\051 model. Although the BDF model) 108 224 P
1.14 (is Turing-complete, it is often possible to construct static schedules by graph clustering) 108 203 P
1.87 (techniques [Buck93], and only when these techniques fail does one resort to dynamic) 108 182 P
-0.1 (scheduling. Even when dynamic scheduling becomes necessary, clustering techniques can) 108 161 P
-0.71 (still reduce the number of modules that have to be scheduled at run-time since significantly-) 108 140 P
-0.47 (sized subgraphs can often be scheduled statically even if the entire graph cannot be. Hence,) 108 119 P
0.17 (a multidimensional extension of boolean dataflow would be desirable as this would allow) 108 98 P
