0.82 (There has been interesting work done at Thomson-CSF in developing the Array-) 144 425 P
2.98 (Oriented language \050AOL\051 [Deme94]. AOL is a specification formalism that tries to) 108 404 P
5.14 (formalize the notion of array access patterns. The observation is that in many) 108 383 P
2.63 (multidimensional signal processing algorithms, a chief problem is in specifying how) 108 362 P
0.88 (multidimensional arrays are accessed. For example, in a 3 dimensional array of data, an) 108 341 P
-0.28 (FFT may be performed on blocks of input data taken in one dimension. Furthermore, these) 108 320 P
0.52 (input blocks may overlap each other. It is observed that the computations themselves are) 108 299 P
2.37 (often no different from similar computations in 1 dimensional signal processing. For) 108 278 P
-0.35 (example, a sum-of-products computations behaves the same way regardless of whether the) 108 257 P
0.07 (dimension of samples it is computing on belongs to the time dimension \050where it is called) 108 236 P
1.03 (FIR filtering\051 or in spatial dimensions \050in which case, the computation is called \322beam-) 108 215 P
(forming\323\051.) 108 194 T
-0.48 (It is assumed that computations are specified by a precedence graph in which actors) 144 173 P
1.56 (represents computations \050called \322elementary transformations\323\051, and the edges represent) 108 152 P
0.76 (precedence constraints. The goal of the AOL formalism is to separate the computational) 108 131 P
0.49 (aspects from the array-access aspects so that computations need not know about how the) 108 110 P
0.67 (data they are operating on is arranged. This is done by associating a \322template\323 for each) 108 89 P
