0.61 (The multidimensional dataflow model presented in the previous chapter has been) 144 500 P
0.34 (shown to be useful in a number of contexts including expressing multidimensional signal) 108 479 P
5.3 (processing programs, specifying flexible data-exchange mechanisms, and scalable) 108 458 P
0.64 (descriptions of computational modules. Perhaps the most compelling of these uses is the) 108 437 P
2.1 (first one: for specifying multidimensional, multirate signal processing systems; this is) 108 416 P
-0.1 (because such systems, when specified in MDSDF have the same intuitive semantics that 1) 108 395 P
-0.63 (dimensional systems have when expressed in SDF. However, the MDSDF model described) 108 374 P
-0.67 (so far is limited to modeling multidimensional systems sampled on the standard rectangular) 108 353 P
1.88 (lattice. Since many multidimensional signals of practical interest are sampled on non-) 108 332 P
-0.43 (rectangular lattices [Mers83][Vaid90], for example, 2:1 interlaced video signals [Dubo85],) 108 311 P
-0.25 (and many multidimensional multirate systems use non-rectangular multirate operators like) 108 290 P
0.37 (hexagonal decimators \050see [Bamb90][Bosv92][Mand93] for examples\051, it is of interest to) 108 269 P
0.23 (have an extension of the MDSDF model that allows signals on arbitrary sampling lattices) 108 248 P
4.55 (to be represented, and that allows the use of non-rectangular downsamplers and) 108 227 P
(upsamplers.) 108 206 T
