1.19 (In SDF, there is only one dimension, and the stream is in that direction. Hence,) 144 684 P
0.94 (whenever the repetitions of a node is greater than unity, then the data processed by that) 108 663 P
0.83 (node corresponds to data along the stream. In MDSDF, only one of the directions is the) 108 642 P
0.43 (stream. Hence, if the repetitions of a node, especially a source node, is greater than unity) 108 621 P
2.07 (for the non-stream directions, the physical meaning of invocations in those directions) 108 600 P
0.21 (becomes unclear. For example, consider a 3-dimensional MDSDF model for representing) 108 579 P
3.17 (a progressively scanned video system. Of these 3 dimensions, 2 of the dimensions) 108 558 P
-0.06 (correspond to the height and width of the image, and the third dimension is time. Hence, a) 108 537 P
1.98 (source actor that produces the video signal might produce something like \050512,512,1\051) 108 516 P
-0.35 (meaning 1 512x512 image per invocation. If the balance equations dictated that this source) 108 495 P
-0.05 (should fire \0502,2,3\051 times, for example, then it is not clear what the 2 repetitions each in the) 108 474 P
0.16 (height and width directions signify since they certainly do not result in data from the next) 108 453 P
0.21 (iteration being processed, where an iteration corresponds to the processing of an image at) 108 432 P
2.31 (the next sampling instant. Only the repetitions of 3 along the time dimension makes) 108 411 P
1.2 (physical sense. Hence, there is potentially room for great inefficiency if the user of the) 108 390 P
0.22 (system has not made sure that the rates in the graph match up appropriately so that we do) 108 369 P
1.05 (not actually end up generating images of size 1024x1024 when the actual image size is) 108 348 P
-0.19 (512x512. In rectangular MDSDF, it might be reasonable to assume that the user is capable) 108 327 P
0.43 (of setting the MDSDF parameters such that they do not result in absurd repetitions being) 108 306 P
2.64 (generated in the non-stream directions since this can usually be done by inspection.) 108 285 P
0.43 (However for non-rectangular systems, we would like to have more formal techniques for) 108 264 P
1.17 (keeping the repetitions matrix in check since it is much less obvious how to do this by) 108 243 P
1.65 (inspection. The number of variables are also greater for non-rectangular systems since) 108 222 P
-0.75 (different factorizations for the decimation or expansion matrices give different solutions for) 108 201 P
(the balance equations.) 108 180 T
