1.31 (The method given above, although much more efficient in practice than a brute) 144 606 P
2.65 (force method, is not efficient in the technical sense of giving us a polynomial time) 108 585 P
3.18 (algorithm for the problem. This is because the complexity of the above method is) 108 564 P
5.46 (dominated by the counting step where there are potentially) 108 543 P
5.46 ( steps. Since) 469.41 543 P
2.51 (, where these quantities were defined before,) 182.03 522 P
2.51 ( is not bounded by a) 430.46 522 P
0.39 (logarithmic function of any of the quantities that appear in the problem. However, unlike) 108 501 P
-0.28 (the brute force method, where counting has to be done in both directions, the counting step) 108 480 P
-0.18 (above is only along the vertical direction and is hence considerably more efficient than the) 108 459 P
(brute force method in general.) 108 438 T
-0.23 (Finally, the method above results in a formula that is messy and very non-intuitive,) 144 417 P
0.69 (and is far from elegant. Perhaps there is no elegant solution to this problem at all, and it) 108 396 P
-0.21 (might even be NP-hard if the counting step turns out to be NP-hard \050we would still have to) 108 375 P
-0.07 (give a reduction\051. Also, the generalization to higher dimensions will only be messier since) 108 354 P
1.25 (we have relied on operations on the individual entries of the matrix, and not on matrix) 108 333 P
(operations. Perhaps an algorithm can be developed, but we do not pursue it here.) 108 312 T
